This video was made possible by... you! Every&nbsp;
sale of our limited edition calendar supports&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:12.680
what we do here at kurzgesagt and our&nbsp;
brand new 12,024 calendar is our most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:18.697
spectacular yet. Don't miss out on your chance&nbsp;
to own a special piece of kurzgesagt history.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.560
The universe is going to die one day. But how?&nbsp;

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:30.662
Well, it turns out, our cosmic fate will&nbsp;
be decided by a fight between two titans.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:34.652
The Two Warriors Deciding the Fate of the Universe

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.720
Our universe was born 14 billion years ago&nbsp;
in the Big Bang and has been expanding ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:44.600
since. For some reason, new empty space&nbsp;
is being created out of nothing between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:51.560
galaxies. Space itself is becoming bigger.&nbsp;
Will it go on forever, or will it stop one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:58.200
day? Today we think the universe is at the mercy&nbsp;
of two cosmic demigods fighting for dominance.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.160
The first one is all the stuff in the universe:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:06.680
galaxies, gas, dark matter. While they are&nbsp;
very different they just want to do one thing:

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:13.000
get together. Matter is attracted by matter,&nbsp;
pulled together by gravity. And gravity also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.840
pulls on the universe as a whole, slowing&nbsp;
the expansion that started at the Big Bang.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:25.000
Our second warrior is empty space. While&nbsp;
it seems, well, empty and powerless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:30.720
empty space has an intrinsic energy. We don’t&nbsp;
really have an idea what this energy is or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:35.800
why it is there. It’s really a placeholder&nbsp;
that fills a blank in our understanding of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:41.760
reality. But it’s got a cool name: dark&nbsp;
energy. Dark energy pushes things apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:46.240
a sort of “anti-gravity” that&nbsp;
accelerates the cosmic expansion.
&nbsp;

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:52.360
So we have all the matter in the universe, pulling&nbsp;
in, and empty space infused with dark energy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:59.840
pushing out. Whoever wins will kill the&nbsp;
universe in fun ways. But who will win?

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:02.600
It all depends on the mysterious dark energy:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:06.360
Will its strength stay the same – a&nbsp;
common assumption just because it keeps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:12.497
our models simple? Or will it get weaker,&nbsp;
or will it get more powerful over time?

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.397
1. Constant Dark Energy – Heat Death

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:24.040
If the strength of dark energy stays constant, it&nbsp;
will win the war. Since space is growing, matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:29.120
is getting more and more diluted, like sugar&nbsp;
in a cup being filled with more and more tea.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:34.640
But as the universe expands, new empty space&nbsp;
is created, which brings more dark energy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:38.600
which pushes everything apart even&nbsp;
more , which creates more empty space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:43.560
which makes the universe grow even faster.&nbsp;
A feedback loop that will make the universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:51.480
expand at an exponential rate. Every 12 billion&nbsp;
years or so it will double in diameter – forever.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.800
But while dark energy is winning the war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:57.640
matter is winning at least one&nbsp;
battle: At short distances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:03.640
it can keep things together. Local galaxy&nbsp;
bubbles can overcome the push of dark energy.

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:08.800
In a few billion years, our local group of&nbsp;
galaxies will merge into a gigantic ball with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:15.120
trillions of stars. It will soon become our last&nbsp;
view of the cosmos. All other galaxies will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:20.560
pushed away by the expansion. For us, it will look&nbsp;
like the rest of the universe is rushing away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:26.080
until in a few hundred billion years we won’t&nbsp;
see other galaxies at all. We will be alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:29.520
surrounded by a seemingly infinite, dark void.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:35.560
In about 100 trillion years, all the stars of&nbsp;
our supergalaxy will have died out. All gas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:41.120
that could create new stars has been consumed,&nbsp;
and no new gas can come in. The galaxy will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:47.320
be dark and filled with stellar corpses. Over&nbsp;
quadrillions of years, white dwarfs and neutron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:54.000
stars will slowly cool until becoming truly dark&nbsp;
– turning off the last lights of the universe.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.680
All structures, big and small,&nbsp;
will slowly dissolve. One by one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:03.640
all dead stars and planets will leave the&nbsp;
supergalaxy, which slowly dissolves over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:09.160
sextillions of years. Every object will&nbsp;
end up on its own, which means that dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:14.040
energy takes over again, creating more&nbsp;
and more empty space between everything.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:19.880
Objects will be so far apart that it will&nbsp;
be as if each had a universe for itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:25.800
Not much happens anymore, until in a googol&nbsp;
years, all black holes will have evaporated.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:31.320
In the end, entropy and dark energy won’t&nbsp;
stop until their job is finished. Over time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:36.000
spans you might as well call forever, all&nbsp;
remaining structures might even dissolve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:41.360
into single particles that will be pushed away&nbsp;
from each other by an ever growing empty space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:47.360
Imagine a whole universe – with just a single,&nbsp;
lonely particle traveling through nothingness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:54.800
This is the final state, the true end. The Big&nbsp;
Freeze, or Heat Death. A completely featureless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.800 --> 00:05:02.441
cold and eternally expanding universe. Nothing&nbsp;
will ever be able to happen again. Forever.

00:05:03.653 --> 00:05:06.104
Well that was a bummer! What else could happen?

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:10.612
2. Increasing Dark Energy – Big Rip

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:16.480
What if dark energy gets stronger? In&nbsp;
this case empty space won’t just win&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:19.800
over matter – it will literally rip it to pieces.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:24.720
In the Big Freeze scenario, matter lost&nbsp;
the war but won some battles. But here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:30.280
matter wins nothing. Dark energy is growing&nbsp;
stronger over time, overcoming the pull of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:36.320
gravity and creating new empty space at smaller&nbsp;
and smaller distances. In this scenario things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:41.920
will escalate quickly – it could start&nbsp;
as early as 20 billion years from now.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:47.360
First, dark energy will create empty space&nbsp;
between individual galaxies. Our galaxy will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:52.960
leave its local cluster and begin to drift alone&nbsp;
in a rapidly inflating and ever darker cosmos.

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:57.720
Some billion years later empty space&nbsp;
starts to push between individual stars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:02.560
dissolving the galaxy. If you live on a&nbsp;
planet in a star system, the night sky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:08.160
will start looking sad and gloomy, as other&nbsp;
stars are pushed too far away to be seen.

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:10.600
A few million years after the sky turns dark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:15.720
dark energy starts to create empty space&nbsp;
inside star systems. Your planet is pushed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:21.560
away from its star and all life in the universe&nbsp;
freezes to death. There is not much time left,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:27.360
as a few months later dark energy is&nbsp;
creating empty space inside solid objects.

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:33.644
Stars, neutron stars, planets, asteroids,&nbsp;
everything solid is being ripped into pieces.

00:06:33.644 --> 00:06:39.280
If you're on a spaceship, you only have a short time&nbsp;
before you are ripped apart. Half an hour later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:44.320
even atoms are destroyed as new space is&nbsp;
being created so furiously that electrons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:50.400
and nuclei are separated. Now the universe&nbsp;
has just a fraction of a second left.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:55.280
In this final moment, only dying black&nbsp;
holes remain, drained and defeated by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.280 --> 00:07:00.080
dark energy. They are tiny, septillions&nbsp;
of times smaller than an atom – and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:05.680
explode with the power of a trillion supernovae&nbsp;
in a trillionth of an octillionth of a second.

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:13.320
Finally, the very fabric of reality is torn to its&nbsp;
core, obliterating spacetime itself. The Big Rip.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:18.987
Space and time have lost their meanings, making&nbsp;
it impossible to predict what will happen next.

00:07:20.138 --> 00:07:21.000
Oof.

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:25.640
3. Decreasing Dark Energy – Big Crunch

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:30.720
Poor matter. But there is&nbsp;
one scenario where it wins:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:35.640
If the strength of dark energy decreases with&nbsp;
time, and if this reduction is strong enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:39.080
the pull of gravity will win and&nbsp;
all the things in existence will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:44.840
move towards each other – unfortunately&nbsp;
making the universe collapse into itself.

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:46.640
No one knows when this might begin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:51.960
but it could be as soon as a few hundred&nbsp;
million years. What will it look like?

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:56.480
As the universe begins to contract, over&nbsp;
billions of years, galaxies and galaxy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:02.000
clusters approach each other until they eventually&nbsp;
collide. They are mostly made of empty space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:06.400
so a collision is like the gentle&nbsp;
merger of two clouds. At any rate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:10.960
first galaxies and later individual&nbsp;
stars get closer and closer.

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:15.400
As the universe goes on collapsing, you might&nbsp;
worry about stars and planets eventually crashing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:20.920
against each other. This will happen, but it’s&nbsp;
not your worst problem. If space itself shrinks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:25.800
this also concentrates all the radiation emitted&nbsp;
in the past by all the stars, supernovae and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:32.640
quasars that ever existed. Now ‘empty’ space is&nbsp;
filled with radiation, the dark nothing between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:39.240
stars is heating up, making life unpleasant and&nbsp;
then impossible as planets just burn. Slowly at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:45.840
first, then rapidly, space gets as hot as it&nbsp;
was after the Big Bang. Stars are pretty hot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:51.680
but now the space around them is hotter&nbsp;
–they are literally boiled from the outside.

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:55.280
As the universe collapses into&nbsp;
itself, all galaxies and all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.280 --> 00:09:01.760
stars merge into a single ball of hot&nbsp;
plasma – the Big Crunch is complete.

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:06.400
From here on there are two possibilities.&nbsp;
Either the universe will collapse completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:11.960
into a singularity, a point of zero size and&nbsp;
infinite density, without space and time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:16.800
The way it might have been before the Big&nbsp;
Bang. Or the universe could “bounce back”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:23.360
restarting the cosmic expansion, creating a&nbsp;
new baby universe. Somewhat poetic really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:27.400
everything died, but everything&nbsp;
is reborn. But to be clear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:32.120
we have zero evidence that this has&nbsp;
happened before or will in the future.

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:34.440
So What Will Happen?

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:38.920
Most scientists think that dark energy will&nbsp;
stay constant, so the likely fate of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:46.320
universe is Heat Death – eternal cold and utter&nbsp;
boredom. Which seems sad but has a huge upside:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:51.960
In this scenario we get to have the universe for&nbsp;
the longest. It gives us trillions of years to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:57.600
expand, jump from star to star, maybe even&nbsp;
from galaxy to galaxy. We might even find a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:03.160
way to keep consciousness around forever. We&nbsp;
don’t know. So we just have to wait and see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:07.126
and make the most of the amazing&nbsp;
universe we have right now.

00:10:09.080 --> 00:10:12.000
Hey you, why don’t you stick&nbsp;
around for a bit longer. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:15.600
want to tell you about something&nbsp;
that changed kurzgesagt forever.

